{"name":"Seaport","hex":"#006e8c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"seaport","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A town or harbour with facilities for seagoing ships to dock and take on or discharge cargo."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seaport"}],"entries":[{"title":"Port","blocks":[{"text":"A port is a maritime facility comprising one or more wharves or loading areas, where ships load and discharge cargo and passengers. Although usually situated on a sea coast or estuary, ports can also be found far inland, such as Hamburg, Manchester and Duluth; these access the sea via rivers or canals. Because of their roles as ports of entry for immigrants as well as soldiers in wartime, many port cities have experienced dramatic multi-ethnic and multicultural changes throughout their histories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/View_of_Nam_Harbor_from_Busan_Tower.jpg/330px-View_of_Nam_Harbor_from_Busan_Tower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/500px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/1280px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/500px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Port of New York and New Jersey, U.S., grew from the original harbor at the convergence of the Hudson River and the East River at the Upper New York Bay.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AManhattan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/La_Lanterna_di_Genova_di_notte.jpg/500px-La_Lanterna_di_Genova_di_n